[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Reading/writing tklib geo/track files}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require {Tcl 8.6-}]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require map::slippy 0.8]
[require snit]
[require map::track::file [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides commands to read and write Tklib [term geo/track] files. The format of these
files is described in section [sectref {File format}], the value returned by
[cmd {map track file read}] and accepted by [cmd {map track file write}] in section
[sectref {geo/track value}].

[para] Packages using these files are [package map::track::store::fs], which loads all
[term geo/track] files of a directory, and through it [package map::track::store::memory].

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd {::map track file read}] [arg path]]

Reads the [term geo/track] file [arg path] and returns its contents as a [term geo/track] value,
see section [sectref {geo/track value}].

[para] If the file cannot be opened, or is not a valid [term geo/track] file, the result is the
empty string. No error is thrown in these cases.

[para] If the file does not specify any name, the track gets a single name, the name of the file
without directory and extension. For example, an unnamed track in file [file /data/walk.track] is
named [const walk].

[call [cmd {::map track file write}] [arg path] [arg track]]

Writes the [term geo/track] value [arg track] (see section [sectref {geo/track value}]) to the file
[arg path], replacing any previous contents. The names are written in dictionary order, with
duplicates removed. The coordinates are written in the order given, and are not validated.

[para] The result of the command is the empty string. Failure to open the file is reported as an
error.

[list_end]

[section {geo/track value}]

A [term geo/track] is a path on the surface of Earth, a polyline, together with a list of names. It
is represented by a Tcl dictionary with the following keys:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const names]]
A list of strings, the names of the track. Several names can be specified, e.g. aliases or names in
different languages.

[def [const geo]]
A list of geographical locations, the vertices of the polyline, in order from one end of the track
to the other. Each location is a list of two numbers, the latitude and the longitude in degrees, in
this order (the [term geo] of [package map::slippy]). Unlike for a [term geo/area] the polyline is
not closed.
[list_end]

For example:

[example {names {{Seawall Walk}} geo {{49.2873 -123.1218} {49.2905 -123.1250} {49.2951 -123.1271}}}]

[section {File format}]

A [term geo/track] file is a text file, read line by line:

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Leading and trailing whitespace of each line is ignored.

[item] Empty lines are ignored.

[item] Lines starting with [const #] or [const //] are comments, and ignored.

[item] The first line which is neither empty nor a comment must be the magic line
[const tklib/geo/track], identifying the file.

[item] Every other line which is not a number is a name of the track. There may be any number of
names, including none. A name cannot look like a floating point number, it would be read as a
coordinate.

[item] Every line which is a number is a coordinate. The coordinates are taken in pairs, the latitude
followed by the longitude of a vertex of the track. The latitude must be in the range -90 to 90,
the longitude in the range -180 to 180 degrees. At least one vertex (two coordinates) is required,
and the number of coordinates must be even.

[list_end]

[para] By convention the names are written after the magic line and before the coordinates, and
files use the extension [file .track]. This is also the default file pattern of
[package map::track::store::fs].

[para] For example:

[example {tklib/geo/track
// Along the seawall, Vancouver
Seawall Walk
49.2873
-123.1218
49.2905
-123.1250
49.2951
-123.1271}]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require map::track::file

map track file write seawall.track {
    names {{Seawall Walk} Seawall}
    geo   {{49.2873 -123.1218} {49.2905 -123.1250} {49.2951 -123.1271}}
}

set track [lb]map track file read seawall.track[rb]
puts [lb]dict get $track names[rb]       ;# -> Seawall {Seawall Walk}
puts [lb]lindex [lb]dict get $track geo[rb] end[rb] ;# -> 49.2951 -123.1271
[example_end]

